'''Sami Jauhojärvi''' (born 5 May 1981) is a Finnish former [[Cross-country skiing (sport)|cross-country skier]] who competed between 2000 and 2017. At the [[2014 Winter Olympics]], he won [[Cross-country skiing at the 2014 Winter Olympics – Men's team sprint|men's team sprint]] with [[Iivo Niskanen]]. Germany launched a protest over the result due to a final-leg collision between Jauhojärvi and [[Tim Tscharnke]], but it was rejected by the jury.<ref name="Reuters protest rejected">{{cite web |url=http://in.reuters.com/article/us-olympics-crosscountry-team-women-idINBREA1I0VE20140219 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306172805/http://in.reuters.com/article/us-olympics-crosscountry-team-women-idINBREA1I0VE20140219 |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 March 2016 |title=Norway women win, Finns cross first in men's race |publisher=Reuters |first=Dmitriy |last=Rogovitskiy |date=19 February 2014 |accessdate=19 February 2014}}</ref> Jauhojärvi's Finland finished fifth in the 4 x 10&nbsp;km relay in [[Vancouver]] in [[2010 Winter Olympics|2010]].<ref>{{cite Sports-Reference|df=dmy-all}}</ref>

Jauhojärvi won his first medal at the [[FIS Nordic World Ski Championships 2009]] in [[Liberec]] with a bronze in the team sprint with [[Ville Nousiainen]], and then added a second in the 4 × 10&nbsp;km relay. Jauhojärvi has won one World Cup race; in Trondheim 2009 he won the 50&nbsp;km classic mass start competition.

He was the 2001 [[FIS Nordic Junior World Ski Championships|Junior World Ski Champion]] in the 30&nbsp;km freestyle at [[Karpacz]].

